        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="16.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#two-platforms" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Two Platforms, Two Jobs</a>
          <a href="#what-40v-buys" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What 40V Buys You</a>
          <a href="#where-18v-enough" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Where 18V Is Enough</a>
          <a href="#overlap" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Overlap Zone</a>
          <a href="#economics" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. The Battery Economics</a>
          <a href="#yard-size" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Sizing It to Your Yard</a>
          <a href="#recommendation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. The Recommendation</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The decision is not really about volts. It is about whether your outdoor work is occasional and light or sustained and heavy, because that determines which battery system you end up living with for a decade.
        </p>

        <div id="two-platforms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Two Platforms, Two Jobs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ryobi runs two major battery systems and they have nothing in common beyond the brand. ONE+ is the 18-volt platform, launched in 1996, spanning hundreds of tools across the workshop, the house and light yard work. The 40V platform is a separate line built specifically for outdoor power equipment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Nothing crosses over. The batteries are physically different and electrically incompatible, the chargers are separate, and there is no adapter that should be used between them. Anyone who buys a 40V mower on the strength of a drawer full of ONE+ packs has bought a mower and no batteries, which is an expensive surprise.
          </p>

          <p>
            The split makes sense once you think about the loads. A drill draws hard for two seconds and then stops. A lawnmower draws hard continuously for forty minutes. Higher voltage means the same power is delivered at lower current, which means less heat in the motor, the wiring and the cells, which is what sustained heavy work demands.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">ONE+ 18V</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">40V</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Launched</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1996</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2014</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Tool count</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very large, 300+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Focused, outdoor equipment</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Covers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Workshop, house, light yard</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mowers, blowers, saws, snow</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Battery cost</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Battery weight</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Best at</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Intermittent, varied work</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sustained high load</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cross-compatible</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            So the question is not which is better in the abstract. It is which one your actual work needs, and whether the answer is one of them or genuinely both.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE CRITICAL FACT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Nothing crosses over. Not batteries, not chargers, not with any adapter.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">This is the most common and most expensive mistake in the Ryobi range. Check the platform badge before buying anything, because the tools sit side by side on the same aisle in very similar green packaging.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-40v-buys" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What 40V Actually Buys You
          </h2>

          <p>
            The advantages are real and they are all consequences of the same physics.
          </p>

          <p>
            Power delivered at lower current. For a given power output, doubling the voltage halves the current, and heating in a conductor goes with the square of current. So a 40V system running a mower generates far less waste heat in the motor windings, the controller and the cells than an 18V system delivering the same power. Less heat means the tool can sustain the load rather than backing off to protect itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sustained runtime under heavy load. A 40V pack carrying a mower through long grass does not sag the way an 18V pack does, which shows up as the blade maintaining speed rather than bogging when the grass thickens. That is the single most noticeable difference in use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Purpose-built tools. The 40V range is designed around outdoor equipment rather than adapted from workshop tools, so the mowers have proper decks, the snow blowers have real augers, and the chainsaws have bar lengths that make sense. The ONE+ equivalents are generally lighter-duty versions of the same idea.
          </p>

          <p>
            Headroom for the jobs that do not compromise well. Snow clearing and larger chainsaw work are the clearest cases: these are either done properly or not worth doing cordless, and 40V is where Ryobi puts the equipment that does them properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The costs are straightforward. The batteries are more expensive and heavier, the platform has far fewer tools so it will never be your workshop system, and if your yard does not actually demand it you are paying for capability you will not use.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-18v-enough" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Where 18V ONE+ Is Genuinely Enough
          </h2>

          <p>
            The ONE+ platform covers more ground than people assume, particularly since the HP brushless range arrived.
          </p>

          <p>
            Everything indoors and in the workshop. Drills, drivers, saws, sanders, multi-tools, vacuums, lights, inflators and the long tail of niche tools. The 40V platform does not compete here at all; it has no drills. If you want one battery system for household work, ONE+ is the only candidate of the two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Light and moderate yard work. String trimmers, hedge trimmers, leaf blowers and pole saws in the ONE+ HP range handle a typical suburban garden competently. For a quarter-acre lot with normal maintenance, these are sufficient and considerably cheaper than their 40V equivalents.
          </p>

          <p>
            Occasional chainsaw work. Limbing, cutting up storm debris, taking down small trees. A ONE+ HP chainsaw with a high-capacity HP pack is a genuine tool for this, if not for felling anything substantial.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small mowing, with caveats. ONE+ mowers exist and work on small flat lawns with regular cutting, and they are miserable on anything large, overgrown or sloped. This is the clearest boundary between the platforms and the section on overlap covers it properly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The strategic argument for ONE+ is the tool count. Three hundred-plus tools on one battery means that whatever niche thing you need next — a caulking gun, a tyre inflator, a fan, a stapler — probably exists on the platform you already own. That compounding convenience is why people stay, and our guide on ONE+ battery interchangeability explains why packs bought today keep working for decades.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="overlap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Overlap Zone, Where the Decision Is Actually Made
          </h2>

          <p>
            Four categories exist on both platforms, and these are where the choice gets made.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tool</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">ONE+ HP Verdict</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">40V Verdict</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Choose 40V If</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">String trimmer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine for most gardens</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">More power, longer run</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Heavy growth, large area</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Leaf blower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Adequate, short bursts</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Substantially stronger</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large property, wet leaves</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chainsaw</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good for limbing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Real cutting capability</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Felling, firewood, big limbs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lawn mower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small flat lawns only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Proper mower</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Anything over a small lawn</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The mower row is the decisive one for most people. Mowing is sustained maximum-load work for thirty to sixty minutes, which is precisely where 18V struggles and 40V is comfortable. If you intend to mow cordless on anything larger than a small flat lawn, that decision effectively selects 40V and everything else follows from it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blowers are the next most common. A ONE+ blower clears a patio and a path perfectly well. Clearing a long drive of wet autumn leaves is a different job, and the 40V versions move meaningfully more air for meaningfully longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chainsaws split on what you actually cut. Limbing and storm tidy-up is ONE+ HP territory. Cutting firewood, felling anything with real diameter, or working through a long session wants 40V, both for the power and because bar length and chain speed matter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Trimmers are the closest call and usually go to ONE+ on cost, unless you are clearing rough ground rather than edging a lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            Work out which of these four you genuinely need before choosing, because in the overlap the platform decision is really a battery decision, and batteries are what you will own for the next decade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="economics" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Battery Economics of Owning Both
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plenty of people end up on both platforms, and it is worth being clear-eyed about what that costs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two platforms means two sets of batteries, two chargers, two sets of spares, and two things to keep charged before a weekend of work. Batteries are the expensive consumable in cordless tools, typically the largest share of lifetime spend, so duplicating them is the main cost of straddling.
          </p>

          <p>
            It also means two ageing curves. Packs degrade whether used or not, so a set of 40V batteries used twice a year for mowing season ages on the calendar regardless, and you replace them having extracted a fraction of their cycle life. Our guide on battery lifespan covers why calendar ageing dominates for occasional users.
          </p>

          <p>
            That said, owning both is genuinely correct for a specific and common case: someone who wants ONE+ for the workshop, where 40V has nothing to offer, and 40V for a lawn that a ONE+ mower cannot handle. Those are non-overlapping requirements and no single platform serves both.
          </p>

          <p>
            What is not sensible is buying into 40V for a trimmer or a blower you could have had on ONE+, because that is where the duplication buys you very little. If 40V is going to be justified, let the mower or the chainsaw justify it, and then put the trimmer and blower on the same platform to share the packs.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Straddle Cost</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Battery sets to buy and maintain</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Chargers and charging routine</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Calendar</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">What ages the seasonal packs</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Mower</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">The purchase that should justify 40V</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="yard-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sizing It to Your Yard
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most reliable predictor of which platform suits you is the property, not the tool list.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
