            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Note that the middle rows are the interesting ones, and they usually resolve into a split: 40V for the mower and possibly the chainsaw, ONE+ for the workshop and the lighter yard tools. That is the common real-world answer for a suburban house with a proper garden.
          </p>

          <p>
            Terrain matters as much as area. A sloped, uneven or heavily treed quarter acre is harder work than a flat half acre, and a ONE+ mower that would cope with the latter will struggle on the former. Be honest about the ground rather than just the acreage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Frequency matters too. Mowing weekly through the season keeps grass short and the load light; letting it grow and then cutting it every three weeks is a much heavier job that an 18V mower will not enjoy.
          </p>

          <p>
            And consider whether cordless is right for the biggest jobs at all. For a large property with substantial mowing, a petrol or ride-on machine is still frequently the better tool, and the cordless decision then only applies to the trimmer, blower and saw — which brings ONE+ back into contention for everything.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recommendation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Recommendation
          </h2>

          <p>
            Condensed to the decisions most people are actually making.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want one platform and your outdoor work is light, choose ONE+. The tool count is the deciding factor: three hundred-plus tools on one battery, including everything indoors, and a compatibility promise going back to 1996 that no competitor matches. For apartments, small gardens and workshop-focused users this is straightforwardly the right answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you need a proper cordless mower, or a chainsaw for real cutting, choose 40V for those and accept that they are a separate system. Do not try to make 18V do sustained heavy outdoor work; it can be made to, and the experience is poor and hard on the batteries.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you own ONE+ already, which most people asking this question do, the default should be to stay on it and only step to 40V when a specific job genuinely demands it. The compatibility promise means every ONE+ pack you own keeps its value, and adding a platform is the expensive move.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you choose, buy into one properly rather than scattering across both. Three good batteries and a rapid charger on one platform beats two mediocre setups, because the constraint in cordless work is almost always having a charged pack when you want one.
          </p>

          <p>
            And check the badge at the till, every time. The 18V and 40V products sit on the same aisle in the same green livery, and the single most expensive mistake in this range is buying the right tool on the wrong platform. Our guide to the best cordless tool brand covers how Ryobi compares against the alternatives if you are choosing a system from scratch rather than adding to one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the Batteries Cross Over</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                They do not, in either direction, and no adapter should be used. ONE+ 18V and 40V are separate systems sharing only a brand and a colour. Buying a 40V mower on the strength of a drawer full of ONE+ packs means buying a mower with no batteries, which is an expensive surprise at the till.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying 40V for a Trimmer or Blower You Could Have on ONE+</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This is where straddling two platforms buys you least. If 40V is going to be justified at all, let the mower or a serious chainsaw justify it, then put the lighter outdoor tools on the same platform so they share those packs rather than requiring a third set.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to Mow a Real Lawn on 18V</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Mowing is sustained maximum-load work for thirty to sixty minutes, which is exactly what an 18V system handles worst. ONE+ mowers are fine on small flat lawns cut regularly and miserable on anything large, overgrown or sloped. This single requirement usually decides the platform.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing by Acreage and Ignoring Terrain</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sloped, uneven or heavily treed quarter acre is harder work than a flat half acre, and cutting every three weeks is far heavier than cutting weekly. Judge the platform on how hard the ground actually is and how often you cut, not on the number on the deeds.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spreading Thinly Across Both Platforms</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Three good batteries and a rapid charger on one platform beats two mediocre setups on two, because the real constraint in cordless work is having a charged pack when you want one. If you genuinely need both systems, commit properly to each rather than under-equipping them.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Ryobi 18V and 40V batteries interchangeable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. They are physically and electrically incompatible, the chargers are separate, and no adapter should be used between them. This is the most common and most expensive mistake in the Ryobi range, because the two lines sit on the same aisle in very similar green packaging. Check the platform badge before buying.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Ryobi 40V worth it over 18V?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For sustained heavy outdoor work, yes. Higher voltage means the same power at lower current, so far less waste heat in the motor and cells, which is what mowing, snow clearing and real chainsaw work demand. For workshop tools and light yard work it buys nothing, and 40V has no drills at all.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mow my lawn with Ryobi ONE+ 18V?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a small, flat lawn cut regularly, yes. On anything large, overgrown or sloped it is a poor experience and hard on the batteries, because mowing is continuous maximum-load work for the better part of an hour. If a proper cordless mower is on your list, that requirement effectively selects 40V.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which Ryobi platform should I buy first?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                ONE+, for almost everyone. It covers the workshop, the house and light yard work across three hundred-plus tools on one battery, with a compatibility promise going back to 1996. Add 40V later only if a specific outdoor job — a real mower, a serious chainsaw, snow clearing — genuinely demands it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Ryobi 40V have drills and workshop tools?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The 40V platform is built specifically for outdoor power equipment: mowers, blowers, trimmers, chainsaws and snow equipment. If you want cordless drills, drivers, saws, sanders and the long tail of workshop tools, that is entirely ONE+ territory and 40V does not compete there.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth owning both Ryobi platforms?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is correct for a common case: ONE+ for the workshop, where 40V offers nothing, and 40V for a lawn a ONE+ mower cannot handle. Those needs do not overlap. What is not worth it is buying into 40V for a trimmer or blower that ONE+ handles, since that duplicates batteries for little gain.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does higher voltage mean more power?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It means power can be delivered at lower current, and since heating rises with the square of current, a 40V system wastes far less energy as heat for the same output. That is why it sustains heavy loads better. It does not automatically make a tool more capable — design and battery quality matter as much as volts.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

